<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-5-number-theory" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction to Number Theory</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Number theory is the branch of mathematics that is concerned with the study of the integers, <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, or more frequently, the positive integers, <m>\mathbb{N}</m>, and the rules and properties associated with them. Though it may seem mundane, number theory is one of the most active fields in research-level mathematics and has a great number of unsolved problems, including what is arguably the most famous unsolved problem in contemporary mathematics, the Riemann Hypothesis.
    </p>

    <p> One of the greatest mathematicians of all time, Carl Freidrich Gauss, was said to have made the following comment: </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Die Mathematik ist die Königin der Wissenschaften und die Arithmetik ist die Königin der Mathematik.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      In English, "Mathematics is the queen of sciences, and arithmetic [number theory] is the queen of mathematics." If that is not a compelling endorsement of the field, I do not know what is. 
    </p>

    <p>
      In this module, we'll build the basics of number theory. We'll talk about divisibility, the prime numbers, the division algorithm, and modular arithmetic. In the next module, we'll dive deeper into the theory and will study the Euclidean Algorithm, modular exponentiation, and Sunzi's Theorem.
    </p>
  </introduction>

